+### What I would change
+
+Use `SafeERC20` for approvals to the Aave pool, not `IERC20.approve` directly. Since USDT is both no-return and has the well-known nonzero-to-nonzero allowance restriction, I would use OpenZeppelin `SafeERC20.forceApprove` for the pool allowance path, or otherwise explicitly reset to zero and perform low-level optional-return-safe approval. I would keep the fork test and require it to pass against the real USDT/Aave addresses before deployment.
+
+## Finding 2 - High: withdrawal fees are stranded in Aave when all shares exit
+
+### What I found
+
+`withdraw()` computes `grossAssets`, subtracts `grossAssets` from `totalAssetsStored`, burns all requested shares, but only withdraws `netAssets` from Aave at `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:111-119`.
+
+If the last depositor withdraws all shares, `totalShares == 0` and `totalAssets() == 0`, but the withdrawal fee remains as aUSDT owned by the vault. There is no fee recipient, sweep function, or accounting path that can redeem it. The README says fees remain supplied and accrue to remaining shareholders, but in the last-shareholder case there are no remaining shareholders.
+
+### How I found it
+
+I added `test_WithdrawFeeIsStrandedAfterAllSharesExit()` in `test/UsdtYieldVaultAccountingFailure.t.sol`. It performs one deposit, withdraws all shares, and asserts that no Aave receipt tokens remain after `totalShares` and `totalAssets` are both zero.
+
+Evidence command:
+
+```bash
+forge test --match-path test/UsdtYieldVaultAccountingFailure.t.sol -vv
+```
+
+Output:
+
+```text
