+## Finding 3 - Medium: `depositFeeBps` has no upper bound and can brick deposits
+
+### What I found
+
+`setDepositFee()` accepts any `uint256` at `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:126-129`, and the constructor also stores `_depositFeeBps` without validation at `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:72-79`.
+
+Values at or above `10_000` bps are not semantically usable:
+
+- `10_000` bps makes `netAmount == 0`, so every deposit reverts with `NoSharesMinted`.
+- More than `10_000` bps makes `amount - fee` underflow and revert with a Solidity panic.
+
+This is owner-only, but it is still value math that decides whether users can deposit. A bad deploy parameter, bad governance action, or compromised owner can halt deposits through a configuration value that the contract presents as valid.
+
+### How I found it
+
+I added `test_DepositFeeCannotExceedBasisPointDenominator()` in `test/UsdtYieldVaultAccountingFailure.t.sol`. The test expects a fee above the denominator to be rejected. The setter accepts it, so Foundry reports `next call did not revert as expected`.
